WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.040
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:03.720
and in a stunning turn of events I find myself in an airport.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:04.800
So I've got this 7-year-old son

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.580
and every Saturday he wakes up at 5 in the morning

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:08.220
and watches 2 hours of Pokémon

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.940
before I can even you know, like, get up and take the iPad away from him.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:13.880
He has all the Pokémon encyclopedias and all these Pokémon cards

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.900
I don't even know where he gets them from,

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.720
I think he trades for them on the schoolyard playground?

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.100
And it's great, y'know, I want my kid to have passions,

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:22.840
I want him to be excited about stuff

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.300
but I know nothing about Pokémon.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.780
Well, aside from what Henry has told me

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:28.460
and a brief foray into Pokémon Go,

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:31.460
so today I though I would explain the world of Pokémon

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.960
from the perspective of someone who has never seen Pokémon.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.800
Alright Hank, so we all know that the last 500 years

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.960
have seen this huge decrease in biodiversity on Earth.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:40.700
Well, good news!

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:43.780
In the near future, we get a big influx of species

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:47.900
and not just any species, like cute, cuddly, awesome, talented species!

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:49.020
They're called Pokémons.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.280
How did they arrive on Earth?

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.100
I have no idea, was it like an alien world Noah's Ark situation?

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.600
Nah, probably not 'cause a bunch of Pokémons are based on current Earth like forms

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.960
like Horsea is, you know, basically a seahorse.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.300
So maybe it was like a radiation event,

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:05.960
point being, there's like hundreds, maybe even thousands of new species.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.280
You can catch them by using these things called Pokeballs,

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.000
like you throw a Pokéball at a Pokémon

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.640
and the little Pokémon ends up inside the Pokéball,

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:14.580
and then you put the whole contraption into your backpack.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.180
Why would you capture all these Pokémon?

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.420
I mean why do people do anything ultimately?

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:19.520
Like why do people fish?

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.500
So you catch all these Pokémon and then you train them

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.280
so that they will be excellent in battle.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.400
Wait, I forgot something important!

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.180
Sometimes you catch like too many of one kind of Pokémon

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:31.500
like you've got 17 Pidgeys and you don't need 17 Pidgeys and it's weighing down your backpack.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.280
In that case I believe you can transfer your Pokémon

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:35.840
to someone called "the professor",

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.640
but this seems like it's maybe not a good idea.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.520
I don't really know what he does with transferred Pokémons

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.600
but I asked Henry about it once and he said,

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.560
"Dad, Pokémons do not like going to the professor."

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.480
Right, so your job as a Pokémon trainer is to train up your Pokémon

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:53.640
and make them strong so that then, they can do battle with other people's Pokémons.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:56.580
From what I can gather, it's basically like a Roman gladiator style thing

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:57.940
but, you know, for children.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:00.580
Also, they don't fight to the death, they fight until they "faint",

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:01.760
but that's not tricking anyone.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:02.860
Like Henry explained to me,

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:05.740
"Dad, 'fainting' is what Pokémons call dying."

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:07.160
And I was like, yeah, no, I get it.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:09.120
So you catch Pokémon with your Pokéballs,

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:10.960
you train them up, you take them to battles,

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.240
and then you fight to the 'fainting' against other people's Pokémon.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:18.220
And as far as I can tell, that's it, like it's basically just a sport?

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:21.000
It doesn't seem like winning Pokémon battles helps save the world

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.040
or make it a more fair or just place,

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.720
but then again, you know, neither does soccer, and I like soccer!

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:28.720
"The game", as Tottenham Hotspurs fans like to put it, "is about glory,"

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.860
which is to say that, you know, in the scheme of things, the stakes are very low.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.240
That said, there's a lot of questions I don't have the answer to,

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:36.540
like how do Pokémon evolve? I have no idea.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.400
If this is identical to fishing, why not just fish?

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:43.400
And, most importantly, given that most Pokémon are based either on legends (like Charizard is a dragon)

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:46.640
or on actual animals (like Rhyhorn is a rhinoceros),

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:48.140
what is Pikachu?

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:50.380
He seems to be just our collective idea of adorable

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:54.480
that we throw balls at and then put inside of a tiny spherical cage in our backpacks.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:59.540
Which is weird, but I guess it gets less weird when you consider that that's our relationship with many non-human animals.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:02.160
So Hank, that's my best attempt to introduce the Pokémon universe

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.640
from someone who knows nothing about the Pokémon universe.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.700
Alright Hank, I have to go get on an airplane, I will see you on Friday.

